⚽
"Football"/en"足球"/zh-CN"足球"/zh-TW
=== start/en
¶A top-down goalkeeping game. You are the glove in front of the goal on the
left. One ball at a time is struck from somewhere down the pitch, aimed at a
spot inside the mouth, and you move up and down to get in its way. The number
counts the shots you have blocked: it stands up tall each time you save one,
and swells and shakes when one gets past you.¶

¶Where a shot is aimed, and where the glove stands. A goal is about eight times
the width of the keeper guarding it, which is why one person cannot simply stand
in the middle of it and save everything.

A @Place is an output's bottom-left corner, not its middle, so everything aimed
or clamped below is figured in centers and gives up half a size when it becomes
a @Place. An emoji also draws a good deal larger than its size — a 0.6m ⚽ fills
about 0.9m, a 1.2m 🧤 about 1.25m — so those halves are measured, not halved
from the size. The glove's speed is how far it slides on each tick.¶
goalX: -7m
mouthEdge: 5.2m
ballHalf: 0.45m
keeperX: -6.5m
keeperHalf: 0.62m
keeperReach: 4.5m
keeperSpeed: 0.55m

tick: Time(50ms)
press: Key(down: ⊤)
release: Key(down: ⊥)

¶Which way the glove is travelling. An arrow pressed sets it going and letting
that same arrow go stops it, so the glove slides for as long as you hold the
key. Across a mouth this wide, a step per press would never get there in time.¶
drift•#: 0 … ∆ press | ∆ release …
	∆ press ?
		((press = 'ArrowUp') ? 1 ((press = 'ArrowDown') ? -1 drift))
		((release = 'ArrowUp') & (drift = 1)) ? 0
			((release = 'ArrowDown') & (drift = -1)) ? 0
				drift

¶The glove is not moved by physics, so it can be steered directly. It stops at
the ends of its reach, so it can never wander off the goal.¶
keeperY•#m: 0m … ∆ tick …
	((keeperY + (drift × keeperSpeed)).max(keeperReach · -1)).min(keeperReach)

¶A save and a goal. Each @Collision reports a touch beginning and a touch
ending, so both are paired with a check that this one is a beginning: without
it every save and every goal would count twice.¶
gloveHit: Collision('ball' 'keeper')
netHit: Collision('ball' 'net')
saved: (∆ gloveHit) & ~(gloveHit•ø)
scored: (∆ netHit) & ~(netHit•ø)

•Shot(sx•#m sy•#m slant•# speed•#m/s)

¶Somewhere to stand, somewhere in the mouth to aim, and the line between them.
Working the slant out from the two ends is what puts every shot exactly on
target, whatever direction it comes in from.¶
ƒ newShot()•Shot (
	x: Random(2m 6m)
	y: Random(-5.5m 5.5m)
	aim: Random(-4.7m 4.7m)
	¶The slant is how much y the run gives up per metre of x. A division can have
	no answer, so the ø case falls back to a shot straight down the pitch; the
	kicker never stands on the goal line, so it never comes up.¶
	drop: (aim - y) ÷ (goalX - x)
	Shot(x y (drop•ø ? 0 drop) Random(16m/s 22m/s))
)

¶1 = the ball waits at the kicker's feet, 2 = it is on its way, 3 = it is being
cleared. Ticks are counted rather than clocks subtracted, since each @Time
starts from zero. The blocks are what you see; the serves are only there to
give the number a new name every shot, which is what replays its animation.¶
•Round(phase•# ticks•# blocks•# serves•# last•# shot•Shot)
round•Round: Round(1 0 0 0 0 newShot()) …
	∆ tick | saved | scored …
	round.phase ???
		1: (round.ticks ≥ 14)
			? Round(2 0 round.blocks round.serves round.last round.shot)
			Round(1 (round.ticks + (∆ tick ? 1 0)) round.blocks round.serves round.last round.shot)
		2: saved ? Round(3 0 (round.blocks + 1) (round.serves + 1) 1 round.shot)
			scored ? Round(3 0 round.blocks (round.serves + 1) 2 round.shot)
				¶A ball that never arrives — knocked back into open play by a save,
				say — is given up on after a few seconds, so the game can never wedge
				waiting for an outcome that isn't coming.¶
				(round.ticks ≥ 70)
					? Round(3 0 round.blocks (round.serves + 1) 3 round.shot)
					Round(2 (round.ticks + (∆ tick ? 1 0)) round.blocks round.serves round.last round.shot)
		3: (round.ticks ≥ 16)
			? Round(1 0 round.blocks round.serves round.last newShot())
			Round(3 (round.ticks + (∆ tick ? 1 0)) round.blocks round.serves round.last round.shot)
		round

¶The one evaluation on which the ball leaves the kicker's foot. It has to be
momentary: it is what strikes the ball, and striking it twice would double its
speed.¶
kicked: (∆ tick) & (round.phase = 2) & (round.ticks = 0)

¶Held still while it waits, struck along its aimed line when it is kicked, and
knocked back upfield by a save or by the back of the net — which is also what
keeps a ball from rattling around inside the goal sounding the net over and
over. The air slows a rolling ball, as grass would, so shots ease in.¶
ballPlace: Motion(
	Place((round.shot.sx - ballHalf) (round.shot.sy - ballHalf))
	nextplace:
		(round.phase = 1) ? Place((round.shot.sx - ballHalf) (round.shot.sy - ballHalf)) ø
	nextvelocity:
		(round.phase = 1) ? Velocity(0m/s 0m/s 0°/s)
			kicked ? Velocity(
					(round.shot.speed · -1)
					((round.shot.speed · -1) × round.shot.slant)
					Random(-45°/s 45°/s))
				(saved | scored) ? Velocity(
						Random(8m/s 14m/s)
						Random(-5m/s 5m/s)
						Random(-45°/s 45°/s))
					ø
)

ball: Phrase('⚽' 0.6m
	name: 'ball'
	place: ballPlace
	matter: Matter(0.45kg 0.7 0.02))

¶The glove needs @Matter to be in the physical world at all, but it is placed
by the program rather than by physics, so it passes through the ball instead of
striking it; the save above is what sends the ball back. Its moves are not
tweened, so it is exactly where you steered it rather than easing after you.¶
keeper: Phrase('🧤' 1.2m
	name: 'keeper'
	place: Place(keeperX (keeperY - keeperHalf))
	matter: Matter()
	duration: 0s)

¶Who is about to shoot, and from where. Only on the pitch while the ball waits.¶
kicker: Phrase('🦵' 1.2m
	place: Place((round.shot.sx + 0.5m) (round.shot.sy - 0.6m))
	duration: 0s)
kickerList: (round.phase = 1) ? [kicker] []

¶The goal: the mouth, the net across the back of it, and a post above and below.
Only the net is named, so only the net can score — a ball that struck a post
would simply bounce. The net is deliberately thick: the fastest shot crosses
about half a metre between physics steps, and a thinner one it could pass
straight through without ever touching.¶
netShape: Shape(Rectangle(-8m mouthEdge -7.2m (mouthEdge · -1)) name: 'net' background: Color(96% 0 0°))
postTop: Shape(Rectangle(-8m 7m -7.2m mouthEdge) background: Color(35% 0 0°))
postBottom: Shape(Rectangle(-8m (mouthEdge · -1) -7.2m -7m) background: Color(35% 0 0°))

¶The touchlines, which keep a cleared ball on the pitch.¶
wallTop: Shape(Rectangle(-8m 7m 8m 6.8m) background: Color(88% 5 130°))
wallBottom: Shape(Rectangle(-8m -6.8m 8m -7m) background: Color(88% 5 130°))
wallRight: Shape(Rectangle(7.8m 7m 8m -7m) background: Color(88% 5 130°))

¶A save: the number pops up a little and stands there a moment before settling.¶
saveFlex: Sequence({
	0%: Pose(scale: 1)
	30%: Pose(scale: 1.5 offset: Place(0m 0.4m))
	65%: Pose(scale: 1.35 offset: Place(0m 0.25m))
	100%: Pose(scale: 1)
} 0.5s)

¶A goal: the number swells to fill the screen and shakes, so a shot getting
past you is never something you could miss.¶
goalShake: Sequence({
	0%: Pose(scale: 1)
	8%: Pose(scale: 3.6 rotation: -10°)
	24%: Pose(scale: 3.6 rotation: 10° offset: Place(0.4m 0m))
	40%: Pose(scale: 3.6 rotation: -10° offset: Place(-0.4m 0m))
	56%: Pose(scale: 3.6 rotation: 8° offset: Place(0.3m 0m))
	72%: Pose(scale: 3.4 rotation: -6°)
	100%: Pose(scale: 1)
} 1s)

¶The count of shots blocked. Its name carries the serve number, so every shot
gives it a new identity and its animation plays again — a number whose name
never changed would animate once and then sit still.¶
scoreView: Phrase("🧤 \round.blocks\" 1.4m
	name: "score\round.serves\"
	place: Place(-1.4m 4m)
	duration: 0.2s
	entering: round.last ???
		1: saveFlex
		2: goalShake
		Pose())

¶The instructions ride on the pitch rather than in the overlay: the overlay's
bottom edge is where the music rendering stands, and text there would be drawn
over.¶
instructions: Phrase("hold ⬆️ ⬇️ to move your glove — block every shot!" 0.55m
	place: Place(-6.4m -6.4m))

¶Three sounds: the strike, the save, the net. Each is put on stage for as long
as the phase that causes it lasts, and taken off again afterwards. Reaching the
stage is reason enough to sound, and a one-shot that has played out stays silent
until it leaves and comes back — so none of these needs a @Music.replay, and
none of them sounds when the program starts.

Being on stage for a whole phase is also what makes them reliable. @Music.replay
asks for a flag true on a single evaluation, and a save is a collision, which is
answered the instant it happens rather than on the next drawn frame; the flag can
rise and fall again before anything reads it. A phase lasts many frames and
cannot be missed.¶
kickSound: Music(
	Track([1] instrument: Instrument.drums beat: 0.25beats volume: 60% loop: ⊥)
	name: 'kick')

saveSound: Music(
	Track([8 12 15] instrument: Instrument.bell beat: 0.2beats volume: 70% loop: ⊥)
	name: 'save')

goalSound: Music(
	Track([5 4 2 1] instrument: Instrument.trumpet beat: 0.3beats volume: 65% loop: ⊥)
	name: 'goal')

outcomeSound: round.last ???
	1: [saveSound]
	2: [goalSound]
	[]

soundList: round.phase ???
	2: [kickSound]
	3: outcomeSound
	[]

Stage(
	[
		ball keeper :kickerList
		netShape postTop postBottom
		wallTop wallBottom wallRight
		instructions
		:soundList
	]
	place: Place(0m 0m -14m)
	frame: Rectangle(-8m 7m 8m -7m)
	background: Color(62% 40 140°)
	color: Color(20% 0 0°)
	gravity: 0m/s^2
	overlay: [scoreView]
)
